= Distributed cognition — Technical =

== Functional-system boundary ==
Distributed cognition expands the analytic unit from individual actor to a system linked by functional relations.

Representational state can propagate across:

<pre>
person → artifact → person → environment → person
</pre>

The transformations across media are part of the cognitive process under analysis.

== Representational propagation ==
External representations differ from internal ones in persistence, visibility, manipulability, and access.

These properties alter error detection, memory burden, coordination, and computational sequence.

== Temporal distribution ==
Cognition can also be distributed through time.

Procedures, records, conventions, and designed artifacts preserve results of prior cognitive work for later actors.

== Method ==
Cognitive ethnography combines observation, artifact analysis, interaction analysis, and representational modeling to reconstruct real-world cognitive systems.

== Boundary caution ==
A broad system boundary should earn explanatory value.

Merely using a tool does not automatically make every task a useful distributed-cognition case.

